<.shell title="Colour" current={:colour}>
  <h1>Colour</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Every colour is a token with a light and a dark value. Both modes are shown side by side; the
    switch in the top bar sets the rest of the guide.
  </p>

  <div class="modes">
    <div
      :for={{mode, label} <- [{"theme-light", "Light"}, {"theme-dark", "Dark"}]}
      class={["mode", mode]}
    >
      <h2 class="first">{label}</h2>
      <div :for={{group, tokens} <- palette()}>
        <h4>{group}</h4>
        <div class="swatches">
          <div :for={{name, light, dark, _use} <- tokens} class="swatch">
            <div class="chipcolor" style={"background: var(--#{name})"}></div>
            <strong>{name}</strong>
            <span>{if mode == "theme-light", do: light, else: dark}</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="example card">
        <p class="paragraph">
          Body text with
          <.a href="#">a link</.a>
          and <span class="text-text-muted">muted text</span>.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
          <.button variant={:primary}>Primary</.button>
          <.button>Secondary</.button>
          <.badge kind={:success}>Active</.badge>
          <.badge kind={:incident}>Incident</.badge>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>In a class list</h2>
  <p>
    A colour is the property and the token's name: <code>text-text-muted</code>,
    <code>bg-surface-alt</code>, <code>border-border-subtle</code>. Every token is there and
    nothing else, so a raw Tailwind colour such as <code>text-zinc-600</code> builds nothing.
    Reach for a class that already says it first: <code>.hint</code> is small muted text.
  </p>

  <h2>What each token is for</h2>
  <%= for {group, tokens} <- palette() do %>
    <h4>{group}</h4>
    <.table id={"tokens-" <> String.replace(String.downcase(group), " ", "-")} rows={tokens}>
      <:col :let={{name, _light, _dark, _use}} label="Token" class="w-px mono whitespace-nowrap">
        --{name}
      </:col>
      <:col :let={{_name, light, _dark, _use}} label="Light" class="w-px mono">{light}</:col>
      <:col :let={{_name, _light, dark, _use}} label="Dark" class="w-px mono">{dark}</:col>
      <:col :let={{name, _light, _dark, _use}} label="Class" class="w-px mono whitespace-nowrap">
        {token_class(name)}
      </:col>
      <:col :let={{_name, _light, _dark, use}} label="Use">{use}</:col>
    </.table>
  <% end %>

  <h2>Rules</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Use tokens, never a hex value, in components and pages.</li>
    <li>
      Text on <code>--primary</code>, <code>--success</code>, <code>--danger</code>, <code>--info</code>, and the D4H
      colours is white. Text on <code>--warning</code> is near-black.
    </li>
    <li>
      Green and red text use <code>--success-text</code> and <code>--danger-text</code>, never the
      fills. In dark mode the fills are too dark to read as text.
    </li>
    <li>
      Red and green always come with a word: <strong class="text-success-text">Add</strong>, <strong class="text-danger-text">Remove</strong>. Colour alone is never the only signal.
    </li>
    <li>Focus is <code>--focus</code> yellow in both modes, so it reads the same everywhere.</li>
    <li>The D4H activity colours match D4H, so people know them on sight.</li>
  </ul>
</.shell>
